WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:25.550
Hi there.

9
00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.290
Today, we're going to talk about garage sale.

10
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.210
We are.

11
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:29.970
And you know what?

12
00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.890
I have to say, it kind of feels like we're sitting in a garage sale.

13
00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.550
It really does.

14
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:42.310
We need to share for a minute, I think, that we are recording on the very last day that Earwolf has its studio.

15
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.670
As we sit here, there are folks moving tables out.

16
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:54.330
There's a whole area where there's just misplaced cups and coffee mugs and different things.

17
00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.990
And it's like, there's a little sign, take if you want it.

18
00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.430
Chairs are being packed up.

19
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.270
I just boxed up my desk.

20
00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.170
My desk that I was so excited to get just a few months ago.

21
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.350
I know.

22
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.890
They're taking pictures off the walls.

23
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.070
It kind of feels like we're sitting in a rummage sale.

24
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:16.050
And I'm a little sad, I have to say, to say goodbye to this place where we, you know, created the podcaSt.

25
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.570
And we had so many wonderful memories in this little studio.

26
00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.970
And truth be told, we don't even know where we're recording next week.

27
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.330
But that's what we're doing this afternoon.

28
00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.770
We're going to try to find a new place to record.

29
00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.510
And the show will go on.

30
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:35.690
And thankfully, me and Jenna and Cassie and Sam, we're all in it together.

31
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:41.790
We will find a place, even if it's just a nice, you know, hangout in one of our garages.

32
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.750
But we're a little sad today.

33
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:45.430
Sad.

34
00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:46.610
Mad.

35
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:48.510
Sad.

36
00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:50.130
Sad mad?

37
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.230
Sad mad.

38
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:51.930
Sad mad.

39
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:52.510
Yeah.

40
00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.990
Jenna does not like change, you guys.

41
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:54.730
No.

42
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:56.410
Makes me sad mad.

43
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.410
Makes you sad mad.

44
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:57.950
All right.

45
00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.930
But this is a wonderful episode.

46
00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:04.910
I just wanted to share with you guys, though, what's going on around us as we record Garage Sale.

47
00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.370
Well, we're sort of sitting in a garage sale.

48
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.030
Well, this episode was Season 7, Episode 19.

49
00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.090
It was written by John Vitti and directed by Steve Carell.

50
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:24.250
I was so tickled to discover that he directed this episode that included such a momentous moment for his character.

51
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:24.850
Yeah.

52
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.350
Wow.

53
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:27.630
I'd forgotten that.

54
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.070
Me too.

55
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:31.130
And then when I saw it, I was like, oh, then I had all these memories, you know?

56
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.290
And he took such care with all of those scenes.

57
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.150
We'll get to them.

58
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.650
Well, here is your summary.

59
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:41.930
In this episode, Dunder Mifflin is throwing a garage sale in the warehouse.

60
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:49.070
Dwight tries to trade his way to the best item in the garage sale, but gets bested by Jim.

61
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.190
Meanwhile, Michael plans to propose to Holly.

62
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.570
However, she might need to move to Colorado to take care of her aging parents.

63
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.350
So let's get into fast fact number one.

64
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.710
This was Amy Ryan's last episode of The Office.

65
00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.210
This was it, her last appearance.

66
00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.630
And here's a fun tidbit.

67
00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:17.430
Holly's first episode that she appears in and her last episode both contain proposals.

68
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:22.550
There's Andy and Angela in her first episode, which was Goodbye, Toby.

69
00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.550
And now Holly and Michael in this one.

70
00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.090
Oh, that's such a great detail.

71
00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:27.750
I know.

72
00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.330
Amy did an interview with Vulture, and she said this about the episode.

73
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:33.110
Quote,

74
00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.730
The script was so sweet that it made us all have a good cry.

75
00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:41.410
Coupled with the fact that it was the start of Steve's departure, and it was my own.

76
00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.010
Yeah, there wasn't a dry eye in the house.

77
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.030
And that was so true.

78
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:46.870
So true.

79
00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.290
And boy, when I watched this episode, Angela, I sobbed.

80
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.530
It came over me.

81
00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.050
I wasn't even expecting it.

82
00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:54.650
Same.

83
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.450
I went back and I read the shooting draft.

84
00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.790
You guys should know, usually when I go back and look at the shooting draft, there's also

85
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.790
the table draft, and there's maybe one or two little rewrites and tweaks, and then we get

86
00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:04.850
to the shooting draft, right?

87
00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:05.230
Mm-hmm.

88
00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.610
This episode was pretty much exactly as it was at the table read.

89
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.150
Wow.

90
00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:10.510
Yeah.

91
00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.030
And there's barely any candy bag alts at all.

92
00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.290
There was like one for Pam.

93
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:17.190
That was it.

94
00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:22.930
But it is, as it was written, it is so beautifully written, I cried reading the shooting draft.

95
00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.350
Angela, do you remember the A.V.

96
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.690
Club writer Miles McNutt, who we talked about last week?

97
00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:29.530
Yes.

98
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.930
How he did not care for Todd Packer?

99
00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.530
No, Miles was not a fan.

100
00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.390
Well, he gave this episode an A-.

101
00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:36.430
Hey!

102
00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:41.070
And he called it a, quote, spiritual successor to Casino Night.

103
00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.590
So Miles really liked this one.

104
00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:44.010
Yeah.

105
00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:53.170
Also, this episode was voted the 20th best overall episode of the entire series by Office

106
00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:54.050
Tally fans.

107
00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.210
And I mean, we made 201 episodes.

108
00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.510
And this was number 20.

109
00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.770
This was a very well-liked episode is what I'm getting at.

110
00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.590
Now, fast fact number two, you know, at the end of this episode, Michael announces that

111
00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.230
he's moving to Colorado with Holly.

112
00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:10.690
Yeah.

113
00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:16.390
Well, following the airing of this episode, the governor of Colorado, John Hickenlooper,

114
00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.850
issued a press release appointing Michael Scott to the position of director of paper

115
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:26.950
distribution in the Department of Natural Resources for Colorado.

116
00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:33.610
He said, quote, Scott's success in selling paper will help Colorado effectively and efficiently

117
00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:38.230
move the large amount of bark beetle lumber from the forest and into the marketplace,

118
00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:41.810
creating tons of jobs and making lots of money.

119
00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:48.510
He also said, quote, this is a unique opportunity to resolve Colorado's forest health and budget

120
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:48.950
issues.

121
00:05:55.290 --> 00:06:01.010
Now, how many people that heard that thought maybe there was someone named Michael Scott

122
00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.270
that was hired and not a television character?

123
00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.450
I know.

124
00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.570
It's so true.

125
00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:12.470
Like, I feel like my grandmother might have been like, oh, yeah, we just got Michael Scott.

126
00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.810
He's going to help with the...

127
00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:14.690
With the paper.

128
00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:15.050
Yeah.

129
00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:20.590
And then, you know, a year later, they're like, what happened with that guy is doing nothing.

130
00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.650
Well, I heard he liked dirty jokes.

131
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:23.610
Exactly.

132
00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:30.890
All right, that brings me to fast fact number three, maybe one of my favorite fast facts

133
00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:38.050
ever, because in this episode, Dwight trades his way from a thumbtack to a pouch of magic

134
00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:38.570
beans.

135
00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.930
Well, to a telescope, but then some legumes.

136
00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:44.870
Yes.

137
00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:51.910
This storyline was based on a true story known to many people as the story of the red paper

138
00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:52.230
clip.

139
00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:59.230
Back in July of 2005, a Canadian blogger named Kyle McDonald started with a red paper clip

140
00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:03.630
that he found on his desk, and he traded his way to a house.

141
00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:09.430
He blogged about his journey, and it became this really famous story, and he even did a

142
00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.610
TED Talk about it, and here's a clip where he explains how it all began.

143
00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:18.650
Ten years ago, I was looking down at my desk, and I saw a red paper clip sitting there, and

144
00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:19.970
I said, you know what?

145
00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:23.770
I remember this game called Bigger and Better, where you start with something small, and you

146
00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:25.790
trade it for something bigger, and then you repeat.

147
00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.390
I wonder what would happen if I took this red paper clip and tried to trade it.

148
00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:34.250
So I posted a picture of that red paper clip on a website called Craigslist, and two girls

149
00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:37.190
named Ronnie and Karina responded and said, hey, that's pretty cool.

150
00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.070
We'd like to trade with you.

151
00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:39.570
We've got a pen shaped like a fish.

152
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.020
I was really excited.

153
00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:43.900
This was a cool pen.

154
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.080
This was bigger and better than a red paper clip.

155
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.500
How far can I go with this idea?

156
00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.780
Anybody want a pen shaped like a fish?

157
00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:51.640
Absolutely.

158
00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.700
My name's Annie, and I've got a doorknob with a crazy face on it.

159
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.760
Two trades in, I've already gone way up from a paper clip, and I was thinking, how far can

160
00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:01.140
I go with this?

161
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.660
Maybe I could keep going until I one day owned a house or something from this.

162
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.000
So he did.

163
00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:07.580
Mm-hmm.

164
00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:10.900
He kept making trades until he got a house.

165
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.480
It is such a great TED Talk.

166
00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:18.040
If you guys haven't seen it, we'll put a link to it in our stories, but it's amazing.

167
00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:19.260
It really is.

168
00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.700
And something that's fun is that with each of the 14 trades, that's how many it took,

169
00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:27.100
he has a picture of himself with each of the people that he traded with.

170
00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.460
So here's what happened.

171
00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.040
He found someone who traded him a camping stove for that crazy doorknob.

172
00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.360
For the camping stove, he got a Honda generator.

173
00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:44.100
He traded the generator for an instant party, which was basically an empty beer keg with a

174
00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.940
promise to fill it up and a neon Budweiser sign.

175
00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:54.120
The instant party got the attention of a radio personality who traded him his worst snowmobile.

176
00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.220
Oh, yeah.

177
00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:58.320
Which was really funny, because I guess the guy had a lot of snowmobiles.

178
00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:00.020
He gave him this crappy one.

179
00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.980
I'll give you my worst one for this instant party.

180
00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:07.620
He traded the snowmobile for a two-person trip to the Rockies in British Columbia.

181
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.780
He traded the trip for a box truck.

182
00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.380
He traded the box truck for a recording contract.

183
00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:21.140
Then he found a woman who had a duplex, and only half of it was rented.

184
00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:28.320
And this woman was like, I will give you one year free rent in my unrented side of my duplex

185
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.000
for that recording contract.

186
00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:31.800
And he was like, great.

187
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:39.160
So when the renter of the other side of the duplex found out, she was like, I would like

188
00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:42.500
a year of free rent in the duplex I'm already living in.

189
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.000
What if I traded you an afternoon with my boss?

190
00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:49.700
And Kyle was like, who is your boss?

191
00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:53.300
Well, it turns out that her boss was Alice Cooper.

192
00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:58.820
And Kyle explained that Alice Cooper's tour manager called him and said, why don't you

193
00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:04.180
come on down, see what an afternoon with Alice Cooper is like, and then you'll know what

194
00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:04.760
you're trading.

195
00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.180
We'll offer one for a trade.

196
00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.620
And the video in the TED Talk is so cool because you see him on stage with Alice Cooper, and

197
00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.380
Alice Cooper brings out a giant red paperclip.

198
00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:17.920
Yeah, he's so into it.

199
00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.140
It's so cool.

200
00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.080
So now here's the crazy part.

201
00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:25.060
This is where it got a little bit like, what?

202
00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:25.660
Yeah.

203
00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.700
He trades the afternoon with Alice Cooper for a kiss snow globe.

204
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.200
Yes.

205
00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.780
But he had done some research.

206
00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:36.060
Yes.

207
00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:42.220
He was able to trade the snow globe for a role in a movie.

208
00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:43.740
And here's how.

209
00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.860
And this was the only time during the entire project where I had another trade lined up.

210
00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:54.060
Every other trade had come along serendipitously and it just been this amazing experience.

211
00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:58.840
However, two months previous to all this, this guy had called me up and said, hey, my name's

212
00:10:58.840 --> 00:10:59.640
Corbin Bernson.

213
00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:00.840
I'm a huge Hollywood actor.

214
00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:06.560
I'm making a movie and I would like to offer a paid speaking credited role in a Hollywood

215
00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:06.940
film.

216
00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.860
Would you be interested in trading for that?

217
00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:12.560
I had just done the recording contract trade and I was like, yes, absolutely.

218
00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:13.540
This sounds perfect.

219
00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.600
He hung up the phone and I'm like, Corbin Bernson, who is this guy?

220
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.680
Turns out he is very well known.

221
00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:19.920
He's been in many major movies.

222
00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:25.140
And he also, according to Wikipedia, has the world's largest snow globe collection, over

223
00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.000
6,500 snow globes.

224
00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.700
Since it was Wikipedia, I knew it was true.

225
00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:33.700
And I just sort of kept it in the back of my head.

226
00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.540
And when Mark said he had a Kiss snow globe, I was like, oh, this is perfect.

227
00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:39.120
Called Corbin.

228
00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:40.260
Corbin, do you want the Kiss snow globe?

229
00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.080
He's like, send me a picture.

230
00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:41.800
I sent him a picture.

231
00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.040
Corbin called back.

232
00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.280
Not only do I want it, I need it.

233
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:52.540
So, yeah, he kind of knew if he could get his hands on a snow globe, he'd be able to

234
00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:53.700
trade it for this movie role.

235
00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.280
A really unique snow globe.

236
00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:56.380
Yes, exactly.

237
00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:02.160
So, now he's got this role in a movie and here's how he trades that for a house.

238
00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:09.180
Following this, the economic development officer of the town of Kipling, Saskatchewan,

239
00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:13.360
a fellow named Burt Roach, called me up and said, we see that you've been doing this

240
00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:18.580
project, our town has a couple extra houses that we own, would there be a potential that

241
00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:21.720
maybe we could trade one of these houses for something you have?

242
00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.460
And I said, well, right now I have a role in the movie.

243
00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:24.500
He's like, oh, that'd be perfect.

244
00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:28.840
What we were thinking is having a huge housewarming party, a huge celebration, inviting everyone

245
00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:33.260
in the world to come to Kipling and we could offer an opportunity.

246
00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:37.740
